##
   A `ptr<T>` value cannot be relabelled as a `ref<T>`.

   This is the forgery this closes. Because dereferencing a reference needs no
   `marked` block, a `ptr` that could be relabelled would make the whole escape
   hatch bypassable by one annotated assignment -- `var r: ref<i32> = p;` and
   then `^r` with no `marked` anywhere.

   It used to be possible, via a one-directional `(Ref, Pointer)` arm in
   `types_match`. It was accepted here and, inconsistently, `f(@x)` was
   rejected. See KNOWN_GAPS.md 4.2.
##
expose func run(): i32 {
  var x: i32 = 7i32;
  var p: ptr<i32> = @x;
  var r: ref<i32> = p;
  return ^r;
}
